<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Nevers</h1><p class="st-meta">990 CE – 1539 CE (approx.) · capital Nevers</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Loire county in Burgundy's orbit that passed through Flemish, Burgundian and Cleves hands before becoming a duchy in 1539.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 990 Otto-William, count of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a>, gave the county of Nevers to his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landry-count-of-nevers">Landry</a>, who founded its first dynasty. Landry's descendants also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonnerre">Tonnerre</a>, giving the counts a large lordship along the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonne-river">Yonne</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nevers lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> between the royal domain and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy</a>, with forests, ironworks and river trade. Several counts went on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-count-of-nevers">Count William III</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>. In the 13th and 14th centuries the county passed by marriage to the Flemish counts, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-count-of-flanders">Louis of Nevers</a>, and then to a younger branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">Valois</a> dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1491 it passed to the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, and in 1539 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a> raised it to a duchy-peerage for François de Clèves. The Duchy of Nevers later went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzagas</a> and the Mazarins, and lost its last rights in the Revolution.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-nevers.jpg" alt="County of Nevers" width="320" height="345"><figcaption>Image: Rocherd · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_France_1477-fr_sovereign_B%C3%A9arn.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>990 CE – 1539 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nevers</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Landry of Nevers, Louis I of Flanders, François I of Clèves</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nevers, Duchy of Nevers</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1264&amp;focus=county-of-nevers" aria-label="County of Nevers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4754 528 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Nevers lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5074 784L5054 728M5074 784L5118 774"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5118 774h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5074 784h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5074 784h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nevers. <a href="/atlas?year=1264&amp;focus=county-of-nevers">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

